{
  "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
  "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: We'll hear argument now in Antonio Dwayne Halbert v. Michigan. Mr. Moran."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE O'CONNOR: Where -- where do we find the waiver here? Is it in the joint appendix?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice BREYER",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is that at issue here?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. MORAN",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What the Michigan constitution does, Justice O'Connor, if I may come back to your question, is it says that for somebody who pleads guilty or nolo contendere, they are giving up the right to the automatic appeal, and they have -- they then have to proceed by the application for leave to appeal. And so the Michigan constitution explicitly still provides a right to appeal, but it changes the method."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Michigan Court of Appeals",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Michigan Court of Appeals denied his appeal, after his futile attempt to -- to identify his issues, with an order saying that there was a lack of merit in the grounds presented."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GINSBURG",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That's just -- but that's just boiler plate, isn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "After Abela v. Martin in the Sixth Circuit. And there is a -- a whole host of Federal district court decisions in the Eastern and Western District of Michigan all saying that when an order is denied for lack of merit in the grounds presented, the AEDPA standard of review applies."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0001-0005",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "They obtain deference on habeas corpus review."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State's amicus",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0001-0006",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I'll just point out that even the State's amicus doesn't agree with that, and -- and rightfully so in light of Swenson v. Bosler, which has rejected an attempt to reorganize by labeling how one's appeals go."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0001-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The State maintains that the appeal to the Michigan Court of Appeals is a second-tier appeal, and that is plainly contrary to the -- to the actual function of that court because there is a provision that if trial counsel suddenly realizes in a plea case, after sentencing, that there are issues that she should have raised, it allows her to do that. And so the State now argues that that's really the first-tier appeal, and then the application that follows after that to the Michigan Court of Appeals is a second-tier appeal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: So, so far as you can tell, that's the way it's been."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0002-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "They issue about 4,000 opinions each year."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0002-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RESTUCCIA: No. They're panels of three."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Then why -- if it's just denying -- or not granting appeal, why does it use the boiler plate, for lack of merit in the grounds presented?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May -- may I ask you one thing lest we forget it? Is -- is your argument premised on the assumption that a refusal to hear an appeal is -- is not regarded as a determination on the merits and thus preclusive?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Does the denial of an application for appeal have any merits consequence?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If it's always on the merits, it's an -- it's an appeal of right, it seems to me. How can you have a -- a discretionary appeal which always decides the merits of the case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Schaerr",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, again, because -- because if you -- if you broaden the right to State-paid counsel, as -- as in Douglas, you're going to require States to -- to reallocate resources to that priority and away from other priorities."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Schaerr",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "subsidize the exercise of a right that they're not required to provide in the first place, like the right to seek review of a -- of a plea-based conviction, then you give the States a strong incentive to cut back on or eliminate that right altogether."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Moran",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0003-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Michigan constitutional provision? It changes the way in which guilty plea appeals proceed. Before 1994, there were other types of appeals that had to proceed by application for leave to appeal, primarily late appeals."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Moran",
      "case_id": "2004-03-10198",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce9fddf05fb871493711c11d44b1b92e01b54464926cab658c92fbf6fbb45589",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce9fddf05fb8-0003-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But all of those appeals are still decided on the merits with preclusive effect for law of the case purposes."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
